WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.750
Yusuf typed one word into a

2
00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:06.150
chatbot: “fractions”.

3
00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:10.010
Back came three pages, and none of

4
00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:10.990
it helped with his homework.

5
00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:14.530
The chatbot had no idea what

6
00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:15.570
he actually needed.

7
00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.250
Today: how to ask an AI for

8
00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:20.930
exactly the help you need.

9
00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:24.350
A chatbot can’t see your textbook,

10
00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.290
your class or your teacher.

11
00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:28.880
It doesn’t know your age, or

12
00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:30.000
what you’ve already learned.

13
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.080
It only knows what you type.

14
00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:35.990
So a vague request gets a vague answer.

15
00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.310
What you type is called a prompt.

16
00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:42.590
A good one says four things.

17
00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:43.750
What you want.

18
00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:45.750
What you already know.

19
00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:46.890
Your level.

20
00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:49.870
And the form you want the answer in:

21
00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:53.470
steps, an example, or a short list.

22
00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:56.710
Here’s Yusuf’s request, rebuilt.

23
00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:58.700
“I’m in Grade seven.

24
00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:00.930
I can add fractions with

25
00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:01.850
the same denominator.

26
00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:06.140
Explain step by step how to add a half

27
00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:08.380
and a third, with one example.”

28
00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:11.550
Same chatbot, a much better answer.

29
00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:14.020
Now the smart part.

30
00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:17.480
Instead of asking for the answer, ask

31
00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.340
it to explain step by step, to give

32
00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.260
you a hint, or to quiz you.

33
00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.230
Then you do the thinking, and the

34
00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:26.730
learning stays with you.

35
00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:29.040
It’s like asking for

36
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:30.360
directions in a new town.

37
00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.440
Ask for “the market”, and someone

38
00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.200
points vaguely down the road.

39
00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.990
Say you’re at the bus station, on foot,

40
00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.670
looking for the vegetable stalls, and you

41
00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:42.270
get directions you can follow.

42
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:44.080
Quick check!

43
00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:47.370
You’re stuck on question four

44
00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:48.790
of your maths homework.

45
00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.520
Which request helps you learn the most?

46
00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.370
A hint for step one.

47
00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:57.940
You still do the thinking, so

48
00:01:57.940 --> 00:01:59.560
question five won’t stop you.

49
00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:01.800
And if the hint isn’t enough,

50
00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:03.280
ask for the next one.

51
00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.470
One more thing: keep personal

52
00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:08.130
details out of your prompts.

53
00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.760
Your full name, address, phone number,

54
00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:14.620
passwords, and photos of you or your

55
00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:16.120
friends don’t belong there.

56
00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:19.330
Some tools keep chats and may use

57
00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:20.730
them to train later models,

58
00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:22.290
unless that’s switched off.

59
00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.420
Ask about the maths, not about you.

60
00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:28.340
In the app, you’ll build a prompt

61
00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:31.620
from five ingredients: role, task,

62
00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:34.160
context, format and language.

63
00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:37.090
Its example asks for questions

64
00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:38.590
about the water cycle.

65
00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:41.170
Add one ingredient at a time,

66
00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:44.250
press “Ask the AI”, and watch the

67
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:45.830
answer get more useful.

68
00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:47.880
Your turn.

69
00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:49.960
Which of these should

70
00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:51.440
never go into a prompt?

71
00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:53.610
Your phone number:

72
00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:56.010
personal details stay out.

73
00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:58.610
Remember: say what you want,

74
00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:01.030
what you already know, your

75
00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:03.250
level, and the form you want.

76
00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:06.790
And ask for hints, steps or a quiz,

77
00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:08.610
so the thinking stays yours.

78
00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.100
Try it yourself: take one

79
00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.060
homework question.

80
00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:15.270
If you’re allowed to use a

81
00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:17.310
chatbot, ask it for the answer,

82
00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.230
then ask for a hint instead.

83
00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:21.210
Compare the two replies.

84
00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:23.540
Which one taught you something?
